; ======================================================================
;  hw_mmc.pi4 - the Pi 4 HwMmc* backend. THE ONE FILE THAT KNOWS THE
;  BCM2711'S SD/eMMC CARD IS DRIVEN BY Lib/emmc.pi4; the core above it
;  (Anvil/Core/mmc_cmd.pbi) knows only the HwMmc* seam and the portable
;  #HW_MMC_* vocabulary.
;
;  This is the board side of the HwMmc* seam declared in Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi.
;  The raw `mmc` command calls these names and never touches a register, a
;  CMDn word, or SdReadBlock directly; this file is thin wrappers over
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/emmc.pi4, whose header carries all the register-level
;  reasoning (the $FE340000 EMMC2 base, the 32-bit-only accesses, the
;  broken-cd handling, and why the base clock has to be supplied).
;
;  DISTINCT FROM #CAP_STORAGE, ON PURPOSE. Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi says it in as
;  many words: HwMmc* is "the raw `mmc` family, distinct from the
;  mounted-filesystem path #CAP_STORAGE rides". `f`/`mount`/`load`/`save`
;  go through RaspberryPi4/Board/storage.pi4, which puts a FAT layer over a
;  block reader and tries USB first. `mmc` goes straight at the SD card as
;  512-byte blocks, no filesystem, no USB - the debugger's view of the
;  medium.
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER, and it is not arbitrary (board.pi4 lists all of these):
;    * RaspberryPi4/Lib/emmc.pi4  BEFORE this file - it defines SdInit,
;      SdReadBlock, SdWriteBlock, SdBlockCount, SdIsSdhc, SdErrorText and
;      #SD_BLOCK_SIZE, which these wrappers ride. A library never includes
;      a library (house rule), so board.pi4 lists both, emmc.pi4 first.
;    * Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi          BEFORE this file - it defines the
;      #HW_MMC_* vocabulary HwMmcType() returns.
;    * RaspberryPi4/Board/storage.pi4  BEFORE this file - it defines the
;      gSdUp bring-up record. THIS IS SHARED DELIBERATELY, see below.
;
;  WHY gSdUp IS SHARED RATHER THAN A SECOND FLAG. SdInit() resets the
;  controller. storage.pi4 already brings the card up once and remembers it
;  in gSdUp so it never pays that cost twice. If this file kept its OWN "is
;  it up" flag, then `f` (mount, which sets gSdUp) followed by `mmc info`
;  (which would see its own flag still 0) would call SdInit() a SECOND
;  time, resetting the card out from under the FAT layer storage.pi4 just
;  mounted on it. One record, one reset. HwMmcUp() reads and sets the same
;  gSdUp storage.pi4 does, so the two paths cooperate instead of fighting.
;
;  THE BASE CLOCK IS 250000 kHz AND IT IS NOT A GUESS. emmc.pi4 refuses to
;  run rather than assume a base clock (#SD_ERR_BASECLK); 250 MHz was
;  measured off the VideoCore mailbox on THIS board and is the same number
;  storage.pi4 supplies. If that ever changes, both call sites change - the
;  value lives at both because emmc.pi4's rule is that the CALLER owns it.
;
;  #CAP_MMC IS 1 ON THIS BOARD, so this real backend is what runs. A board
;  that declares #CAP_MMC = 0 provides no HwMmc backend and RequireCap in
;  Anvil/Core/mmc_cmd.pbi refuses the command before any of these are
;  reached - so these wrappers never have to defend against being called on
;  a board with no card slot.
;
;  WRITE IS EXPOSED, AND HERE IS THE HONEST BOUNDARY.
;  --------------------------------------------------
;  Lib/emmc.pi4 HAS a write path: SdWriteBlock (CMD24), a full peer of
;  SdReadBlock. So HwMmcCanWrite() returns 1 on this board and
;  HwMmcWriteBlock rides SdWriteBlock. A board or driver with no write path
;  would return 0 from HwMmcCanWrite() and the `mmc write` command refuses
;  cleanly and loudly - it never silently no-ops. That is the whole reason
;  HwMmcCanWrite() exists as a seam and is not just hard-coded in the core.
;
;  NOT HARDWARE-VERIFIED, IN EITHER DIRECTION. emmc.pi4's header is blunt:
;  the code has executed on silicon but NO SD CARD HAS EVER ANSWERED IT -
;  the bench boots from USB. So neither the read path NOR the write path
;  here has ever moved a byte to or from a real card. Everything in this
;  file is COMPILE-VERIFIED ONLY and a real transfer is owed a Pi 4 with a
;  card in the slot. Reading is safe to attempt on a live card; writing is
;  destructive by definition, which is why the core makes the operator ask
;  for it by name and guards the RAM side hard.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMmcUp - bring the SD card up if it is not already, and report whether
;  a card is ready. 1 ready, 0 not (the reason is in HwMmcErrorText()).
;
;  Idempotent through the SHARED gSdUp record - see the header. The first
;  call pays the reset/identify cost; every call after it is a flag read.
;  emmc.pi4 never prints, so this is silent: the core prints the context
;  and, on failure, the sentence HwMmcErrorText() returns.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMmcUp()
  If gSdUp <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ; The base clock the controller cannot discover for itself. Measured,
  ; not guessed - see the header.
  SdSetBaseClockKhz(250000)
  If SdInit() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  gSdUp = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMmcInfo - the seam's named entry point. Brings the card up (through
;  HwMmcUp) and returns 1 if the card is ready to be queried and read,
;  0 otherwise. The getters below are meaningful only after this returns 1.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMmcInfo()
  ProcedureReturn HwMmcUp()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Geometry.
;
;  HwMmcBlockSize is the transfer unit, always 512 on this driver
;  (Lib/emmc.pi4 #SD_BLOCK_SIZE - SDHC/SDXC are fixed at 512 and CMD16
;  sets SDSC to match). HwMmcBlockCount is the capacity in those blocks, or
;  0 when the CSD could not be decoded (which emmc.pi4 treats as "unknown",
;  not an error) - the core renders 0 as "unknown" rather than "empty".
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMmcBlockSize()
  ProcedureReturn #SD_BLOCK_SIZE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwMmcBlockCount()
  ProcedureReturn SdBlockCount()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMmcType - the card class, normalised to the portable #HW_MMC_*
;  vocabulary so the core never learns "CCS" or "SDHC".
;
;  SdIsSdhc() is 1 for a block-addressed card (SDHC or SDXC) and 0 for a
;  byte-addressed one (SDSC). That is the distinction that actually matters
;  to a reader of raw blocks, so it is the one the vocabulary carries.
;  Before a successful HwMmcInfo() the answer is #HW_MMC_UNKNOWN.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMmcType()
  If gSdUp = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_MMC_UNKNOWN
  EndIf
  If SdIsSdhc() <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_MMC_SDHC
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #HW_MMC_SDSC
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMmcReadBlock - one 512-byte block from the card at lba into *buf.
;  1 on success, 0 with the reason in HwMmcErrorText(). *buf must be
;  4-byte aligned and non-null; emmc.pi4 refuses otherwise (#SD_ERR_ALIGN
;  / #SD_ERR_NULL_BUF), and the core pre-checks alignment for a clearer
;  message before it ever gets here.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMmcReadBlock(lba.i, *buf)
  ProcedureReturn SdReadBlock(lba, *buf)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMmcCanWrite - 1 if this backend can write a block, 0 if it cannot.
;
;  THE GATE FOR THE ENTIRE `mmc write` PATH. On this board Lib/emmc.pi4
;  supplies SdWriteBlock, so this is 1. A future board whose driver is
;  read-only returns 0 here and the core refuses `mmc write` with an
;  honest sentence rather than pretending. This is the seam that lets the
;  same core command be correct on both kinds of board.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMmcCanWrite()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMmcWriteBlock - one 512-byte block from *buf to the card at lba.
;  1 on success, 0 with the reason in HwMmcErrorText(). Same alignment
;  rule as the read. Callers MUST have checked HwMmcCanWrite() first; on a
;  backend that cannot write this would not exist, so there is no
;  "unsupported" return here - the refusal happens one level up, at the
;  gate, before a destructive command is attempted at all.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMmcWriteBlock(lba.i, *buf)
  ProcedureReturn SdWriteBlock(lba, *buf)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMmcErrorText - the address of a NUL-terminated English sentence for
;  the last emmc.pi4 failure. Rides SdErrorText() unchanged; it names the
;  STAGE, and the core prints it with UartWriteStr (it is a POINTER - see
;  the Print/formatter trap documented all over this tree).
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMmcErrorText()
  ProcedureReturn SdErrorText()
EndProcedure
